{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`docs/exit-codes.md` declares itself (`:5-6`) \"the single source of truth for ... which\ncode each subcommand emits\", and states (`:8-12`) that\n`tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_exit_codes.py` \"cross-checks this file's per-command\ntable against the route registry, so a route with no row here ... fails the build.\"\n\nThat guarantee holds only for **top-level** routes. It does not hold for nested\nsubcommands, which is where most of the table's rows live.\n\n## Evidence\n\n`_live_route_names()` (`test_exit_codes.py:246-249`) builds\n`{route.name for route in ROUTES if not route.retired}`. Measured on the live registry:\n\n    'store' in live          -> True\n    'build-vectors' in live  -> False\n    'footprint' in live      -> False\n    len(live)                -> 53\n\nThe parity test's own docstring is accurate and says so plainly: \"each table\nrow begins with a live top-level route. Nested invocations share that route.\" The\n`orphaned` check keys on `row.split()[0]` (`:264`), so a row for any `store <member>` is\naccepted because `store` is live, and the `undocumented` check (`:259-263`) can never\ndemand a row for a member because no member name is in `live`.\n\nNet effect: a new nested subcommand can land with no row, and nothing fails. Observed\ndirectly -- `rebar store build-vectors` (ticket 0461-09ce-4850-42a7) passed `make verify`\ntwice with no row in the table. The gap was caught by an LLM code-review advisory, not\nby the build.\n\nThe table nonetheless carries a row for every other `store` member, so the convention is\nreal and maintained by hand while the doc describes it as enforced.\n\n## Why this matters beyond one missing row\n\nThis is the same defect class the file already documents about itself at `:12-15`:\n\"Until 2026-09 this file claimed a stronger guarantee than any test made, and the table\nhad drifted in both directions while carrying that claim.\" That correction narrowed the\nclaim on the *exit-value* axis (those columns are hand-authored) but left the\n*row-coverage* claim unqualified, and on the nesting axis it is still too strong.\n\nA doc that overstates its own enforcement is worse than one that states none: a reader\nwho trusts the claim has no reason to check the table by hand, which is exactly how the\n`build-vectors` row came to be missing.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `docs/exit-codes.md` no longer claims a guarantee the parity test does not make:\n      the row-coverage sentence states that enforcement covers top-level routes and\n      that nested-subcommand rows are maintained by convention -- OR the parity test is\n      extended so the claim becomes true.\n- [ ] If the enforcement route is taken, a test fails when a live nested subcommand has\n      no row, demonstrated by a mutation (remove one existing member row; the test\n      must fail) and by the inverse (an invented member row names a real nested\n      invocation or the test fails).\n- [ ] If the documentation route is taken, the narrowed wording is shown to be accurate\n      against `_live_route_names()` as measured, not asserted.\n- [ ] `make verify` passes.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nThe missing `store build-vectors` row itself -- that is fixed in 0461-09ce-4850-42a7's\nown change, which is where the omission arose.\n\n## Implementation plan\n\nCites re-measured against this worktree on 2026-11-01; `_live_route_names` is at `:246`,\nnot `:247` as the Evidence section above originally said.\n\n### A second route registry the Evidence section missed\n\n\"No member name is in `live`\" is correct for `ROUTES`. But `src/rebar/_cli/_registry.py`\nbuilds and exports a **second** tuple beside it:\n\n    ROUTES: tuple[Route, ...] = _build_routes()        # :441\n    STORE_ROUTES: tuple[Route, ...] = _store_routes()  # :532 (builder at :444)\n\nBoth are in `__all__` (`:24-35`). Measured:\n\n    len(ROUTES) -> 53 ; len(STORE_ROUTES) -> 9\n    sorted(r.name for r in STORE_ROUTES) -> build-vectors, compact, doctor, export,\n      footprint, fsck, fsck-recover, import, maintenance\n\n`route.group` is a capability bucket (`reads_init_only`, `lookups`, ...), **not** a parent\ncommand, so qualifying `ROUTES` by group yields nothing; `STORE_ROUTES` is the only\nmachine-readable registry of nested members. Enforcement for `store` is therefore cheap.\n\n### Scope limit, measured\n\nOf 82 table rows, 19 are nested, by parent:\n\n    store 10, bridge 3, check 2, deps 1, list 1, resolve 1, show 1\n\nOnly `store` has a registry; the other 6 parents dispatch through their own parsers with\nnothing to check against. Enforcement covers 9 nested rows and cannot cover the other 10.\n\nThis plan therefore takes **both** routes the ACs offer as alternatives: enforce where a\nregistry exists, narrow the wording where none does. Taking only the enforcement route\nwould leave the doc's claim still false for the 10 unenforceable rows.\n\n### Changes\n\n1. `_live_route_names()` (`:246-249`): union in `f\"store {r.name}\" for r in STORE_ROUTES\n   if not r.retired`, keeping the existing `not retired` filter. Returns 62 names.\n\n2. `orphaned` (`:264`) currently keys on `row.split()[0]`, which would let an invented\n   `store no-such-member` row pass and so would not satisfy AC2's inverse. Replace with:\n   for a row whose first token is a parent that has a registry, require the two-token\n   prefix to be live; otherwise keep first-token matching. Measured consequence: the one\n   row with no matching route, `store compact --all`, still passes because its two-token\n   prefix `store compact` is live.\n\n3. Update the parity test's docstring, which reads \"each table row begins with a live\n   top-level route\" -- accurate today, wrong after change 1.\n\n4. `docs/exit-codes.md:10-12`: state what is actually enforced (top-level routes plus\n   `store` members) and that rows under the other nested parents are convention.\n\n### Demonstration (AC2)\n\n- Mutation: delete the `store compact` row; `undocumented` must report `store compact`\n  and the test must fail. Restore, confirm byte-exact.\n- Inverse: add a row `store no-such-member`; after change 2 `orphaned` must report it and\n  the test must fail. Both mutants are run and shown failing, not argued.\n\n### Ordering\n\n`store build-vectors` has no row on `origin/main`; 0461-09ce-4850-42a7 adds it. Change 1\nmakes a missing member row a failure, so this change must land **after** 0461 or it\nbreaks the build. Not a blocker on starting work -- only on pushing.\n\n### Files\n\n`tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_exit_codes.py`, `docs/exit-codes.md`.\n"}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793644005090833251,"uuid":"56c8a12f-e3b6-4030-863a-69f8660e10cb"}